    {"id":"docs_architecture_document_ingestion_source_boundary_documentingestionservice","label":"DocumentIngestionService (inbound port)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\document-ingestion-source-boundary.md","source_location":"§4.2 Port 1","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Single application use-case every adapter (upload, Keka, S3, Azure) calls; owns identity, validation, checksum, dedup, retention decision, row write, and dispatch, so provider knowledge never leaks past the seam."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_document_ingestion_source_boundary_documentcontent","label":"DocumentContent (re-openable byte access port)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\document-ingestion-source-boundary.md","source_location":"§4.2 Port 2, §9.3","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"open() must be re-openable, not a one-shot buffer, so a non-retained document can still be reprocessed by re-fetching from its source instead of making platform retention mandatory and quietly defeating the privacy requirement."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_document_ingestion_source_boundary_originaldocumentstore","label":"OriginalDocumentStore (outbound blob-storage port)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\document-ingestion-source-boundary.md","source_location":"§4.2 Port 3, §11.2","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"storage_reference must become an outcome (nullable) rather than an input the route invents, and nothing downstream may parse it, so 'do not copy this tenant's documents into platform MinIO' is a configuration value rather than a fork of the pipeline."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_document_ingestion_source_boundary_documentsource_port","label":"DocumentSource (deferred pull-side port)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\document-ingestion-source-boundary.md","source_location":"§4.1, §7","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Upload is a push source (bytes arrive unbidden); Keka/S3/Azure are pull sources (enumerate, decide, fetch). One interface serving both would force upload to fake discover()/check_connection(), so the pull contract is kept separate and deliberately not built yet."},

    {"id":"docs_architecture_external_data_source_architecture_sourceconnector","label":"SourceConnector (provider adapter contract: check_connection/discover/fetch)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\external-data-source-architecture.md","source_location":"§7","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Three methods only; sync (leasing, checkpointing, idempotency, failure isolation) stays with the engine so each provider does not reimplement it differently and wrongly."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_external_data_source_architecture_external_documents_ledger","label":"external_documents identity ledger","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\external-data-source-architecture.md","source_location":"§6.3","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Kept as a separate table rather than columns on documents because an external object can exist pre-ingestion, documents are soft-deleted (a unique index there would block re-ingest), sync bookkeeping is not document metadata, and content-addressed reuse makes the relationship many-to-one."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_external_data_source_architecture_change_detection_ladder","label":"Change-detection ladder (version / timestamp / content-hash rungs)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\external-data-source-architecture.md","source_location":"§10.2","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Rungs are guarded on nullable capability declarations, not assumed, so a provider with no timestamps or versions (Keka) falls through to content-hash as a supported configuration rather than a degraded one."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_external_data_source_architecture_credential_provider","label":"CredentialProvider indirection (credential_ref resolution)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\external-data-source-architecture.md","source_location":"§14","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Credentials are never stored in the database, only a locator; resolution happens once per run in the engine so a connector has no API with which to ask for another tenant's secret. Satisfies AGENTS.md invariant 3 against secrets in config/database rows."},

    {"id":"docs_architecture_tenant_pluggable_data_architecture_five_boundaries","label":"Five-boundary frame (document acquisition, blob, relational, vector, business-DB query)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\tenant-pluggable-data-architecture.md","source_location":"§0, §5","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"The five boundaries are not equally worth building; treating them as five instances of the same pattern is named as the main programme risk. Document acquisition and blob storage are small, high-value ports; relational persistence should be relocated, not abstracted; vector needs a reshaped existing protocol; business-DB query is a new retrieval capability, not an adapter."},
    {"id":"docs_architecture_tenant_pluggable_data_architecture_p3_relational_relocation","label":"P3 — Derived relational persistence: connection routing, not a repository abstraction","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\architecture\\tenant-pluggable-data-architecture.md","source_location":"§6 P3, §9","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"entity_views.py generates DDL at runtime (CREATE TABLE/ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS), relational_projection.py uses ON CONFLICT/GREATEST upserts, and provisioning clones templates via pg_tables — a vendor-neutral abstraction would have to reproduce all of that per engine, which is 'a second product'; pointing the identical schema at a different PostgreSQL server is far cheaper and is the recommended path."},

    {"id":"docs_decomposition_02_cap2_connection_control_plane","label":"CAP-2 — Tenant-Scoped Connection Control Plane","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\decomposition\\02-tenant-self-service-data-sources.md","source_location":"CAP-2","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Foundation capability; extends the existing public integration-profile seam into a tenant-admin lifecycle for exactly two approved Azure provider types, with server-side tenant capability resolution so caller-provided identifiers are never authority."},
    {"id":"docs_decomposition_02_cap3_azure_blob_sync","label":"CAP-3 — Durable Azure Blob Synchronization and Source Reconciliation","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\decomposition\\02-tenant-self-service-data-sources.md","source_location":"CAP-3","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Routes through NormalizedDocument/DocumentIngestionService rather than a second pipeline; retains Blob bytes only in temporary working storage so no durable original copy accumulates in MinIO."},
    {"id":"docs_decomposition_02_cap4_external_postgresql_query","label":"CAP-4 — Contract-Governed External PostgreSQL Query Path","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\decomposition\\02-tenant-self-service-data-sources.md","source_location":"CAP-4","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Kept distinct from platform SQL chat; a live-schema fingerprint must match the accepted contract before every query so drift blocks execution rather than silently returning wrong results."},
    {"id":"docs_decomposition_02_cap5_admin_portal","label":"CAP-5 — Tenant Data Source Administration Portal","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\decomposition\\02-tenant-self-service-data-sources.md","source_location":"CAP-5","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Consumes only safe backend contracts from CAP-2 through CAP-4; never renders secrets, connection strings, provider errors, SQL, or database rows."},
    {"id":"docs_decomposition_02_cap6_local_compose_delivery","label":"CAP-6 — Local Compose Delivery, Migration, and Operational Evidence","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\decomposition\\02-tenant-self-service-data-sources.md","source_location":"CAP-6","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Makes the integrated feature locally deployable under the single approved dev Compose environment; no staging/production claim is introduced, matching ADR-014's explicit deferral of those environments."}
  ],
